+### Removing secrets
+
+In an initialised project, remove a secret from the selected environment. This example assumes
+that `staging` already exists; `DEMO_OBSOLETE_KEY` is a synthetic name to replace with the key
+you intend to remove:
+
+```sh
+sotto --env staging rm DEMO_OBSOLETE_KEY
+```
+
+The removal affects only that environment and remains local until you push. If you want to
+synchronise it with the server, log in and push the same environment:
+
+```sh
+sotto login
+sotto --env staging push
+```
+
+On another device that already has this project, log in and pull that environment to receive
+the removal. Push and pull synchronise the environment, not just this one secret:
+
+```sh
+sotto login
+sotto --env staging pull
+```
+
+In interactive use, omitting the name opens a secret picker. Removal normally asks for
+confirmation, with No as the default; declining or cancelling leaves the secret unchanged.
+`--yes` / `-y` skips only that confirmation, not an omitted-name picker or any required unlock
+prompt. The picker and confirmation require interactive stdin, stdout and stderr, a terminal
+other than `TERM=dumb`, and CI not being active.
+
+In non-interactive use, a name is required. A named removal does not ask for confirmation,
+even without `--yes`; do not rely on a confirmation prompt in scripts or pipelines.
+
+Removal hides the secret from ordinary reads and listings (`sotto get` and `sotto ls`) but
+retains its version history. It is not secure erasure and does not revoke the credential at
+its external provider. For recovery options, see `sotto history --help` and
+`sotto rollback --help`.
+
